#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060591} :activity/id #uuid "83dac894-319c-5863-b45c-982a3ff6f9ff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988 :topic/id #uuid "773e9f74-2606-5a7c-96ea-1dbef24812bb" :topic/math-academy-id 176} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060910 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd458eeb41ee09d6a4f58bc343575d46e3d362dc3edca40a47e1a7381fcc9bd2.png)\n\nExpressed in component form, our vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=-2\\mathbf{i} + 4 \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude (length) of $\\mathbf{a}$, we can use the Pythagorean theorem on $\\triangle OXA$.\n\nThe legs of the triangle have lengths\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}OX^(→) & &= \\end{vmatrix}- 2|= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}XA^(→) & &= \\end{vmatrix}4|= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{\\begin{vmatrix}OX^(→) & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}XA^(→)\\right|^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c0b8c99-c0fd-5bb5-b00d-033a3d589c6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2918 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5b84d0dd-ba79-58a3-b448-a38e7315fb69" :step/math-academy-id 15728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060911 :tutorial/content "We just saw that the magnitude of the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nwas given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a_{x} \\\\ a_{y}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid$ to denote the magnitude of a vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "60a2e291-183e-53fd-ae24-4840833f4b38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2919 :tutorial/title "General Formula"} :step/id #uuid "3e7af8b4-e759-5d78-9a44-b80a9db8a7f0" :step/math-academy-id 15729 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059527 :question/id #uuid "e2b211fe-d37a-55dd-bf76-9ffab2b6f128" :question/math-academy-id "e-4297"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6b4ebe4-9baa-5ff0-847a-49d99ba69795" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048882}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501374} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501373}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given in Component Form"} :step/id #uuid "f09b880a-a7cb-5551-8c97-2db4d49a85e4" :step/math-academy-id 15730 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059528 :question/id #uuid "d4ad6011-24a4-5642-997f-dcc4e807224f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4298"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bc170fa-a70e-55af-8f3e-f4ea07431e30" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501415} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501385} {:db/i